---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Docs-as-Code | Mike Kelley"
  description="Mike Kelley's docs-as-code experience: migrating SaltStack documentation from Sphinx and LaTeX to Jekyll and PrinceXML, Git-based reviews, CI/CD publishing, and an Astro site deployed through GitHub Actions."
>
  <section class="page-hero wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">DOCUMENTATION ENGINEERING / CI/CD</p>
    <h1>Docs-as-code in practice</h1>
    <p class="lede">Documentation belongs in a repeatable engineering workflow. I've migrated publishing systems, reviewed contributions through Git, and connected accepted changes to automated builds. This portfolio uses the same approach.</p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button primary" href="#saltstack">SaltStack migration</a>
      <a class="button secondary" href="#portfolio">How this site ships</a>
      <a class="button secondary" href="#quality-gates">Quality gates</a>
      <a class="button secondary" href="https://github.com/MikeKelley89/MikeKelley89.github.io" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View site repository ↗</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap" id="saltstack" data-toc-section>
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">PROFESSIONAL EXPERIENCE / SALTSTACK</p>
      <h2>From Sphinx and LaTeX to Jekyll and PrinceXML</h2>
      <p>At SaltStack, I helped move the documentation platform away from a Sphinx/LaTeX toolchain and into a Git-centered workflow using Markdown, Jekyll, and PrinceXML. The point wasn't simply changing generators. It was making documentation easier to contribute to, review, build, and publish alongside software development.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-grid">
      <article class="feature-card">
        <span class="feature-number">01 / SOURCE</span>
        <h3>Git-based contributions</h3>
        <p>Documentation changes could arrive as pull requests from SaltStack developers or members of the open-source community. That put content changes into a familiar review-and-merge process instead of a separate publishing silo.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="feature-card">
        <span class="feature-number">02 / OUTPUT</span>
        <h3>Jekyll and PrinceXML</h3>
        <p>Markdown and Jekyll supported web documentation, while PrinceXML provided a path to print-ready PDF output. The workflow separated maintainable source content from its published formats.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="feature-card">
        <span class="feature-number">03 / DELIVERY</span>
        <h3>Builds after merges</h3>
        <p>When I reviewed and merged an accepted pull request, the integration could trigger a CI/CD documentation build. Changes from engineers and community contributors could move into generated output without a separate manual publishing cycle.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <div class="architecture-flow" role="img" aria-label="Developer or community pull request, review and merge, automated documentation build, published HTML and PDF">
      <article class="architecture-node"><span>01</span><strong>Contribute</strong><p>Developer or community member opens a pull request</p></article>
      <div class="architecture-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
      <article class="architecture-node"><span>02</span><strong>Review and merge</strong><p>Evaluate the content and merge approved changes</p></article>
      <div class="architecture-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
      <article class="architecture-node"><span>03</span><strong>Build and publish</strong><p>CI/CD generates documentation outputs</p></article>
    </div>
    <p class="small-note">This diagram summarizes the workflow, not the exact historical CI configuration.</p>
    <p><a class="text-link" href="https://docs.saltproject.io/salt/user-guide/en/latest/topics/installation.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">See a public Salt installation guide I worked on ↗</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="section band" id="portfolio" data-toc-section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">THIS PORTFOLIO / LIVE EXAMPLE</p>
        <h2>The same approach with Astro and GitHub Pages</h2>
        <p>This website is source-controlled, built automatically, and deployed from a reproducible pipeline. I edit the Astro source and check changes into the <code>main</code> branch. GitHub Actions handles the build and deployment.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="pipeline">
        <article><span>01</span><h3>Commit source</h3><p>Changes to Astro pages, layouts, and styles are pushed to <code>main</code>.</p></article>
        <article><span>02</span><h3>Run GitHub Actions</h3><p>The push triggers the repository's build-and-deploy workflow.</p></article>
        <article><span>03</span><h3>Generate the site</h3><p>Node dependencies are installed and <code>npm run build</code> generates static output in <code>dist/</code>.</p></article>
        <article><span>04</span><h3>Deploy to Pages</h3><p>The generated artifact is uploaded and deployed to GitHub Pages.</p></article>
      </div>
      <p class="small-note">This repository currently deploys on pushes to <code>main</code> or a manually started workflow. It does not claim to have PR preview deployments or branch-specific documentation versions configured.</p>
      <p><a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/MikeKelley89/MikeKelley89.github.io/blob/main/.github/workflows/deploy.yml" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read the actual GitHub Actions workflow ↗</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap" data-toc-section>
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">ARCHITECTURE OPTIONS</p>
      <h2>Other ways to structure the workflow</h2>
      <p>There isn't one correct docs-as-code pipeline. The right design depends on the release model, contributors, output formats, and how tightly documentation must track the product. These are alternative patterns I would consider, not claims about features currently running on this site.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-grid">
      <article class="feature-card">
        <span class="feature-number">01 / REVIEW</span>
        <h3>PR validation and previews</h3>
        <p>Build every pull request, run link and style checks, and publish a temporary preview so reviewers can inspect rendered pages before merging. Reserve production deployment for the protected branch.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="feature-card">
        <span class="feature-number">02 / RELEASES</span>
        <h3>Versioned documentation</h3>
        <p>Build documentation from release tags or maintained branches, publish versions side by side, and define how fixes are backported to supported product releases.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="feature-card">
        <span class="feature-number">03 / MULTI-OUTPUT</span>
        <h3>One source, multiple formats</h3>
        <p>Keep content in Markdown or structured source, then produce a searchable web site and styled PDFs from the same reviewed revision, with output-specific templates where needed.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="feature-card">
        <span class="feature-number">04 / CODE INTEGRATION</span>
        <h3>Docs alongside the product</h3>
        <p>Keep API descriptions, examples, and developer guides near the code. Require documentation updates in feature PRs and generate API reference from versioned schemas when appropriate.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="feature-card">
        <span class="feature-number">05 / CONTRIBUTOR MODEL</span>
        <h3>Open-source contribution gates</h3>
        <p>Accept outside PRs with contributor guidance, ownership rules, automated checks, and maintainer approval. Keep build credentials and deployment permissions out of untrusted PR jobs.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="feature-card">
        <span class="feature-number">06 / DEPLOYMENT</span>
        <h3>Staging and promotion</h3>
        <p>Build once, test the artifact in staging, and promote that same artifact to production after approval instead of rebuilding different content for each environment.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>


  <section class="section band" id="quality-gates" data-toc-section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">PIPELINE INTEGRATIONS / QUALITY GATES</p>
        <h2>Automated checks before publication</h2>
        <p>A docs-as-code pipeline can validate more than whether the site builds. I would combine deterministic linting and standards checks with AI-assisted editorial feedback, then route the findings into pull-request reviews. These are integration options, not features currently enabled in this portfolio's deployment workflow.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="feature-grid">
        <article class="feature-card">
          <span class="feature-number">01 / LINTING</span>
          <h3>Content and structural linting</h3>
          <p>Run Markdownlint, Vale, link validation, and code-example checks on changed files. Catch broken links, heading hierarchy problems, malformed Markdown, and common writing issues before a reviewer spends time on them.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="feature-card">
          <span class="feature-number">02 / STANDARDS</span>
          <h3>Style guides and STE compliance</h3>
          <p>Apply organization-specific terminology, preferred wording, and style rules through Vale or custom checks. For projects requiring ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English, use a dedicated ruleset or STE-aware checker to flag vocabulary and sentence-structure issues for qualified human review. Automated checks support compliance; they do not certify it.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="feature-card">
          <span class="feature-number">03 / AI REVIEW</span>
          <h3>AI-assisted editorial feedback</h3>
          <p>Use an AI reviewer to comment on clarity, missing prerequisites, ambiguous instructions, audience fit, and consistency with nearby content. Provide the relevant style guide and approved source context, and return suggestions in the PR rather than silently rewriting content.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="feature-card">
          <span class="feature-number">04 / TECHNICAL ACCURACY</span>
          <h3>Schema and example validation</h3>
          <p>Validate JSON, YAML, OpenAPI specifications, and executable examples against the product where practical. A page can read well and still be wrong if an endpoint, parameter, or command has changed.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="feature-card">
          <span class="feature-number">05 / ACCESSIBILITY</span>
          <h3>Rendered output checks</h3>
          <p>Check generated HTML for accessibility issues, missing image descriptions, invalid markup, and navigation problems. Run these checks against built pages, not only source files.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="feature-card">
          <span class="feature-number">06 / FEEDBACK</span>
          <h3>Actionable PR annotations</h3>
          <p>Report errors as required checks and advisory findings as review comments. Give contributors file-and-line context, a rule reference, and a proposed fix. Keep human approval responsible for technical meaning, exceptions, and final publication.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <div class="architecture-flow" role="img" aria-label="Pull request runs linting and style checks, AI-assisted review, then human approval before merge and publication">
        <article class="architecture-node"><span>01</span><strong>Pull request</strong><p>Contributor submits a documentation change</p></article>
        <div class="architecture-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
        <article class="architecture-node"><span>02</span><strong>Automated feedback</strong><p>Lint, style, STE, technical checks, and AI suggestions</p></article>
        <div class="architecture-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
        <article class="architecture-node"><span>03</span><strong>Human approval</strong><p>Resolve findings, merge, build, and deploy</p></article>
      </div>
      <p class="small-note">I would make reproducible errors such as broken links and invalid schemas blocking checks. AI-generated recommendations would remain advisory until a reviewer verifies them. Sensitive or proprietary content should only be sent to approved AI services under appropriate data-handling controls.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section band" data-toc-section>
    <div class="wrap contact-strip">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">THE PRACTICAL TAKEAWAY</p>
        <h2>Documentation as part of delivery</h2>
        <p>Docs-as-code is about more than Markdown in Git. It brings documentation into the same habits that make software delivery reliable: reviewable changes, clear ownership, automated checks, repeatable builds, and traceable releases.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button primary" href={`${base}writing/`}>Writing samples</a>
        <a class="button secondary" href={`${base}resume/`}>Career history</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
